    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with the hierarchy for coding atypical dermatological presentations. We're seeing a lot of cases with irregular, mottled pigmentation and unusual textural changes that don't quite fit the standard L-code classifications for common dermatitis. Can you clarify the protocol for deciding between a specific highly-detailed code versus a more general 'unspecified' code when the morphological features of the lesion are atypical or don't follow a standard pattern?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For atypical dermatological presentations, you must first consider the *most* specific code that captures the *most* prominent features, even if the overall picture is unusual. If the morphology is truly bizarre, a thorough review of the diagnostic criteria for less common conditions, perhaps those involving pigmentary disorders or inflammatory processes, is necessary. If, after exhaustive review, the features remain completely unclassifiable or defy standard categorization, then and only then should you resort to the 'unspecified' code. Remember, specificity is key, even when the presentation is a genuine outlier.",
