    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so if we have a patient where the skin looks almost \"gnarled\" or has these very irregular, jagged-looking growths that don't seem to follow any known dermatological pattern, is there a way to document that \"irregularity\" in the clinical notes that actually helps with the coding? basically, how do we describe those more extreme, non-standard textures so the auditor doesn't just flag it as a mistake?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To document such extreme, non-standard textures, you need to move beyond simple adjectives like \"irregular\" and use precise, descriptive terminology that hints at the underlying pathology. Instead of just saying \"gnarled,\" try terms like \"verrucous,\" \"exophytic,\" \"reticulated,\" or \"indurated,\" depending on whether the growth is rough, like a wart, or hard and thickened. These more technical descriptors act as signposts for the coder, signaling that you are describing a specific, albeit rare, morphological pattern.\n\nWhen you are describing these features, you are essentially trying to build a narrative that justifies the need for a less common or more complex code, rather than the generic \"unspecified\" bucket. A good description helps the auditor see that you have thoroughly examined the lesion and are not just guessing. This level of detail is crucial for coding complex presentations.",
      "source": "target",
